        <div class="card" style="margin-top:2rem">
          <h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:1.2rem;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:1rem">When do you need a discount calculator?</h2>
          <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:1rem">Calculating the final price of an item during a promotion or markdown is one of the most practical daily math tasks. Here are common situations where this calculator helps you make smarter purchases:</p>
          <ul style="color:var(--muted);font-size:0.9rem;line-height:2;padding-left:1.5rem;margin-bottom:1.5rem">
            <li><strong>Major retail sales events</strong> — instantly figure out actual savings during Black Friday, Cyber Monday, seasonal clearances, or Prime Day</li>
            <li><strong>Coupon and promo codes</strong> — determine your total bill when applying percentage-off discount codes during online checkout</li>
            <li><strong>In-store shopping trips</strong> — compare discounted options quickly on your mobile phone while browsing physical stores</li>
            <li><strong>Budgeting and expense planning</strong> — track exactly how much money you save on discounted essentials or big-ticket items</li>
            <li><strong>Business purchasing</strong> — calculate wholesale volume discounts, trade markdowns, or supplier price reductions</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:1.2rem;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:1rem;margin-top:0.5rem">How to calculate a discount manually</h2>
          <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:1rem">To calculate a discount manually without a calculator, you can use these straightforward math steps:</p>
          <ul style="color:var(--muted);font-size:0.9rem;line-height:2;padding-left:1.5rem;margin-bottom:1.5rem">
            <li><strong>Calculate total savings:</strong> Multiply the original price by the discount percentage, then divide by 100. Formula: <code>Savings = Original Price × (Discount % / 100)</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Calculate final sale price:</strong> Subtract the total savings from the original retail price. Formula: <code>Sale Price = Original Price - Savings</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Quick mental trick (e.g., 20% off):</strong> Find 10% of the price by moving the decimal point one place to the left, then double that amount to find 20%.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:1.2rem;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:1rem;margin-top:0.5rem">Percentage discounts vs flat rate markdowns</h2>
          <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:1rem"><strong>Percentage discounts (%)</strong> reduce the original price proportionally. The higher the base price, the larger the actual dollar amount saved.</p>
          <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:1rem"><strong>Flat rate discounts ($)</strong> subtract a fixed dollar value regardless of the product's original price. These are common with instant cash-back offers or rewards vouchers.</p>

          <h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:1.2rem;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:1rem;margin-top:0.5rem">How stackable or double discounts work</h2>
          <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.8">When stores offer an additional discount (such as 20% off plus an extra 10% coupon), percentages do not simply add together to equal 30%. The second discount applies to the price that was already reduced by the first discount, resulting in a true combined discount of 28% rather than 30%.</p>
